  "answer_text": "Great question \u2014 and honestly, there's no single \"correct\" answer. Here's a breakdown of the main pronunciations:\n\n---\n\n## How to Pronounce \"Doge\"\n\nAccording to Wikipedia, \"doge\" can be pronounced **\"dohzh\"** (/do\u028a\u0292/), **\"dohj\"** (/do\u028ad\u0292/), or **\"dohg\"** (/do\u028a\u0261/).\n\nHere are the main camps:\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udfc6 \"Dohzh\" (most common in media) \u2014 /do\u028a\u0292/\nThis is the pronunciation most commonly heard from the media. It rhymes roughly with \"beige\" or \"rouge.\" This is also how the **historical Venetian title** (the chief magistrate of Venice) is often pronounced in English.\n\n### 2. \"Dohj\" \u2014 /do\u028ad\u0292/\nThe pronunciation \"dohj\" was confirmed by Dogecoin's co-creator Jackson Palmer, who said the name was inspired by the animated series *Homestar Runner*, in which a character spells out D-O-G-E and another reads it as \"dohj.\"\n\n### 3. \"Dohg\" \u2014 /do\u028a\u0261/\nThis pronunciation follows the logic that the original word \"dog\" has a hard G, so the silent E only modifies the vowel \u2014 the same way \"slop\" becomes \"slope\" and \"mop\" becomes \"mope,\" making \"dog\" become \"doge,\" pronounced like \"rogue.\"\n\n### 4. \"Doh-geh\" \u2014 /do\u028a\u0261e\u026a/\nThis version uses a long O, hard G, and a spoken \"e\" sound like at the end of \"ballet,\" and is preferred by some because it sounds like a bastardized version of \"doggy.\"\n\n---\n\n## So What's the \"Right\" Answer?\n\nSince \"Doge\" is a word that was practically made up for the purposes of an internet meme, it's no surprise that there's a lot of confusion. Language columnist Ben Zimmer said he would favor /do\u028a\u0292/ or /do\u028ad\u0292/, but noted that pronunciations of words can change, and both the hard G (like \"dog\") and the soft G are in common use and can be justified with reasonable explanations.\n\n**TL;DR:** The most widely accepted and creator-endorsed pronunciations are:\n- **\"Dohzh\"** (rhymes with *beige*) \u2014 most common in media\n- **\"Dohj\"** (rhymes with *lodge*) \u2014 endorsed by Dogecoin's creator\n\nEither of these will be understood and is considered acceptable!",
